.TH WALK 1
.SH NAME
walk \- walk a path
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B walk
[
.B -udftx
] [
.B -n
.IB mind , maxd
] [
.B -e
.I statfmt
] [
.I name ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Walk
prints each
.I name
and, for a directory, every file below it, one a line.
With no
.IR name ,
it walks the current directory, and prints its files' paths without a leading
.BR ./ .
.PP
The options are:
.TF "-n mind,maxd"
.TP
.B -d
Print only directories.
.TP
.B -f
Print only files that are not directories.
.TP
.B -t
Print only temporary files (with
.B DMTMP
set).
.TP
.B -x
Print only files with execute permission.
.TP
.B -u
Do not buffer the output.
.TP
.BI -n " mind,maxd"
Print only what is at least
.I mind
and at most
.I maxd
levels below each
.IR name ;
either may be left out.
.TP
.BI -e " statfmt"
Print, for each file, the fields of its directory entry
.IR statfmt 's
letters name, in that order, separated by blanks:
.B p
the path,
.B n
the name,
.B s
the length,
.B m
and
.B a
the modification and access times,
.B U
the owner,
.B G
the group,
.B M
who last modified it,
.B x
the mode in octal,
.B q
the qid,
.B D
and
.B T
the device and its type.
The default is
.BR p .
.PD
.SH EXAMPLES
The files below the current directory, without the directories:
.IP
.EX
walk -f
.EE
.PP
The ten largest files below
.BR /sys/src :
.IP
.EX
walk -f -e sp /sys/src | sort -nr | sed 10q
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/walk.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR du (1),
.IR ls (1)
